# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill it in. Never commit .env.local.
#
# THIS FILE IS FOR LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY.
#
# `npm run dev` and `npm run build` read `.env.local`. The DEPLOYED shop does
# not: `next.config.ts` sets `output: "standalone"`, and the standalone server
# reads no `.env` file of any kind. In production every value arrives as a real
# process environment variable, and the two kinds arrive by different routes:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_* ........ Docker BUILD ARGS. `next build` inlines them into the
#                          bundles, so they are fixed when the image is built.
#                          Changing one needs a REBUILD and a redeploy, not a
#                          restart. Set them as GitHub Actions Variables, or
#                          pass `fly deploy --build-arg NAME=value`.
#
#   everything else ...... FLY SECRETS: `fly secrets set NAME=value`. Read at
#                          request time, so changing one needs only a restart.
#
# NEVER pass a secret as a build arg. Build args are recorded in the image
# history and readable by anyone who can pull the image.
#
# SETUP.md Step 5 is the runbook. `.dockerignore` excludes `.env*` from the
# build context, this file included, so nothing here ever reaches an image.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Supabase (database + accounts) ----------------------------------------
# Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → Data API / API Keys
# Deployed: both of these are BUILD ARGS.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Server-only. Bypasses row-level security, so it must NEVER be prefixed with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ and never appear in client code. Used by the Stripe webhook.
# Deployed: FLY SECRET.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Stripe (payments) ------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe dashboard → Developers → API keys. Use a test key (sk_test_) until you
# are ready to take real money. Deployed: FLY SECRET.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_

# There is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY here any more.
# This file used to list one and NO CODE IN THIS REPO HAS EVER READ IT.
# Checkout is redirect-based: `/api/checkout` creates a Stripe Checkout Session
# with the secret key server-side and the browser is redirected to Stripe's own
# hosted page, so the client never needs a publishable key. Listing it made
# people set it, and then wonder which of their keys was doing nothing.
# (`@stripe/stripe-js` is likewise in package.json and imported by no file —
# a cleanup candidate recorded in WORKLOG.md §6. Leave package.json alone until
# someone removes it and re-runs the build.)

# From `stripe listen` locally, or the webhook endpoint's signing secret in
# the Stripe dashboard once deployed. Deployed: FLY SECRET.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_

# --- Site ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Used for Stripe redirects, OAuth callbacks and canonical URLs.
# Local: http://localhost:3000 — Production: your live origin.
#
# Deployed: BUILD ARG, and the one that behaves least like you would expect.
# MEASURED: Turbopack constant-folds this read into the server bundle — the
# literal ends up inside `.next/server/chunks/lib_stripe_ts_*.js` and the
# `process.env` lookup is gone from the compiled output. At runtime the
# variable is IGNORED: booting the built server with a different value still
# emits the value it was built with, and booting it with the variable removed
# does not throw, it serves the baked one. So changing the shop's domain is a
# REBUILD, not an env change and a restart. Setting it as a Fly secret does
# nothing whatsoever.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Email (Resend) ---------------------------------------------------------
# Two variables, one capability: RESEND_API_KEY + EMAIL_FROM. Set BOTH or
# NEITHER. Together they are `isEmailConfigured()` (lib/email.ts), and that is
# now the ONLY thing the shop reads — there is no separate public "claim"
# switch, so what a page says and what the sender can do cannot disagree.
# Either one missing = every send returns not_configured and nothing leaves
# the app. Deployed: both are FLY SECRETS.
#
# What sends on those two ALONE:
#   * the order-confirmation email from the Stripe webhook. Set the two
#     secrets and confirmations start going out.
#
# What needs those two AND NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL (see Shop details below),
# because there is nowhere to forward the message otherwise:
#   * contact-form enquiries and newsletter sign-ups. Nothing persists either
#     one, so the send IS the delivery: a failed send loses the message for
#     good. NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL is listed as optional below — it is not
#     optional if you want the contact form to work.
#
# With them unset the shop says so rather than promising anything: no mail of
# any kind is sent, the contact form is replaced by the channels that do work,
# and the pages state plainly that no order email goes out. A customer still
# gets their order number on screen and can still track an order.
#
# Server-only API key from resend.com -> API Keys. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# --- Australia Post -----------------------------------------------------------
# Postage Assessment Calculator key. Free, self-serve and issued instantly from
# developers.auspost.com.au — no ABN, no account, no approval.
#
# This is a RUNTIME value: `fly secrets set AUSPOST_API_KEY=...`. Never a build
# arg and never NEXT_PUBLIC_ — Australia Post's CORS policy blocks browser calls
# anyway, so it has no business in the client bundle.
#
# Without it the shop quotes postage from a deliberately pessimistic fallback
# table instead of the live API. Nothing breaks; the quotes are just rounded up.
AUSPOST_API_KEY=

RESEND_API_KEY=
# The From: address. Must be on a domain you have verified in Resend.
EMAIL_FROM=Bam Studio <hello@yourdomain.com.au>

# --- Error reporting (Sentry) ----------------------------------------------
# ONE variable turns error reporting on. Deployed: FLY SECRET.
#
# Without it the shop behaves exactly as it always has: every failure is a
# console.error on a 512 MB Fly machine whose logs are not retained, and the
# first anyone hears about a confirmation email that did not send is the
# customer asking where their order is. Nothing is queued, nothing retries and
# nothing is kept — with no DSN there is no network call at all, so an absent
# value costs nothing and breaks nothing.
#
# With it set, these are reported: any unhandled error in a route handler,
# server action or server render (instrumentation.ts); a Stripe webhook that
# fails after the customer has paid; a payment taken for a cancelled order,
# i.e. a refund owed; a confirmation email a provider refused; and a database
# read or write that failed where the code deliberately swallows it.
#
# NEVER prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_. Not because the DSN is secret — Sentry's
# browser SDK ships one to every visitor by design — but because reporting here
# is server-side only, and a DSN in the client bundle would mean browser events
# and therefore a third-party ingest host in the Content-Security-Policy that
# next.config.ts derives from what this app actually loads. Server-only costs
# the CSP nothing. lib/observability.ts argues the whole trade, including why
# there is no @sentry/nextjs dependency.
#
# Get one from sentry.io -> Projects -> your project -> Client Keys (DSN). The
# free tier is 5,000 errors a month; the reporter caps itself well under that.
# Format: https://<key>@<org>.ingest.<region>.sentry.io/<project id>
# A value that is set but malformed is logged once and then treated as unset.
SENTRY_DSN=

# Optional. Tags every event so a laptop's errors do not sit in the same list
# as the shop's. Absent: falls back to NODE_ENV ("production" on Fly,
# "development" locally). Deployed: FLY SECRET.
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=

# Optional. The build these errors came from — a commit sha, a date, anything
# stable. Absent: events carry no release at all, which is honest but means
# every deploy's errors group together and "which build broke it" cannot be
# answered. Deployed: FLY SECRET.
SENTRY_RELEASE=

# --- Rate limiting (Upstash Redis) -----------------------------------------
# TWO variables, one capability. Set BOTH or NEITHER — one without the other is
# read as neither. Deployed: FLY SECRETS. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never build args:
# the token is a bearer credential for the database.
#
# Without them, rate limiting is a Map in one process's memory, exactly as it
# has always been. It works — Fly runs one always-on machine — but it resets on
# every restart and every deploy. That matters most at POST /api/track, which
# returns a customer's postal address for an order number plus the matching
# email, and order numbers are a public sequence plus four hex characters. An
# attacker who can provoke a restart gets their guessing budget back for free.
#
# With them set, the counters for /api/track, /api/contact and /api/newsletter
# live in Upstash and survive restarts, deploys and a second machine. Checkout,
# shipping quotes, search suggestions and the confirmation page are still on the
# in-process path — one `await` each, see lib/rate-limit.ts.
#
# If Upstash is slow or unreachable the shop does NOT fail open and does NOT
# fail closed: it falls back to the in-process limiter, which is what it has
# been running on all along. The store is given 500ms and a circuit breaker, so
# an Upstash outage can never hang a checkout. That decision is argued in full
# in lib/rate-limit.ts.
#
# Free tier, no card: console.upstash.com -> Create Database (pick ap-southeast
# so it is next door to the Sydney machine) -> REST API. Copy both values from
# there; they are shown together.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# --- Shop details (optional; shown in the footer and contact page) ---------
# Deployed: ALL BUILD ARGS. Each one is baked into the bundles, so changing any
# of them means a redeploy, not a restart.
#
# Optional for the footer, but REQUIRED for contact-form enquiries and
# newsletter sign-ups to reach anyone — see the email block above.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ABN=

# Leave unset (or "false") until you actually register for GST — while it is
# false the shop shows no GST component, because charging or displaying GST
# you don't collect is a misrepresentation. Set to "true" on the day you
# register (turnover above $75,000).
NEXT_PUBLIC_GST_REGISTERED=false
NEXT_PUBLIC_INSTAGRAM_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_TIKTOK_URL=
